{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== RESIDUAL-GAP DISPOSITION SWEEP (pre-epic-close) ===\n\nCompleted by the coordinating session after the S3 executor agent died on a stall watchdog mid-audit, immediately after T3 landed. Every enumerable gap this run produced gets exactly one disposition below.\n\nFILED (6 tickets, all linked):\n1. smalltime-rapid-elk (2e1f) discovered_from S1 T1 — lifecycle alerts can never dedupe: is_deduped compares a timestamp_ns no caller stamps. Currently in_progress.\n2. best-kingly-monkey discovered_from S2, relates_to RP-04 S7.3.a — retarget RP-04's config reads to binding_lifecycle.py. NOW CLOSED: RP-04 acted on it, and its C3f commit message states outright 'This does NOT reach whole-tree EMPTY: the two binding_lifecycle.py rows remain (RP-04 S7.3.a's retarget)'. The soft relates_to plus a filed ticket was sufficient; no cross-root blocking link was needed.\n3. captious-hennish-mangabey discovered_from S2 T3 — integration idempotency oracle patches binding_store_mod._now_iso but clear_absent now resolves it from binding_lifecycle, so the patch no longer reaches it. In_progress.\n4. easygoing-tremendous-frogmouth discovered_from S3 — preview and dry-run passes perform remote add_label/set_entity_property writes during create recovery: the call's only guard is 'if not scoped_ids' and it receives the real configured transport, while the invariant-filing gate beside it composes both axes. S3 deliberately pinned that gate UNCHANGED rather than widening scope to fix it. In_progress.\n5. insectile-modest-martin discovered_from S3, relates_to both RP-03 S5 tickets — preserve the pre-observation repair boundary when reconcile.py is extracted. Open.\n6. frangible-irongray-adouri discovered_from this epic — the packaged rebar release omits _engine_support/commit_impact.py, so close_precheck._check_file_impact_vs_diff raises ImportError on ANY close with recorded file_impact when running an installed build, AND the process still exits 0, so an automated caller records a close that never happened. Hit while closing S3; worked around by closing from a worktree on the checkout's venv build.\n\nFIXED IN SCOPE (false documented claims corrected rather than implemented — nine total across the epic; implementing them would have injected unrequested behaviour change into pure extractions):\n- 'safety-abort on ambiguous double bind' — bind_confirm is last-writer-wins with no abort anywhere. S2's plan AND its parent story AC both asserted it; both corrected, and the term reserved for S3 where the abort is genuinely new.\n- comment map 'never remapped' — the gate is equality-only, so a differing id overwrites. Docstring corrected; a byte-comparison test that could not fail was replaced with a write-counting oracle and re-verified RED under mutation.\n- rich-emission per-emit save/dirty-gating — the caller never saves per emit and save() is unconditional. AC corrected to 'performs NO save of its own on ANY path'.\n- apply_handlers' justification that binding_store.py 'sits at the module-size cap and cannot carry a narrower accessor' — false once S2 T3 added note_rich_emit; rewritten in T3.\n- binding_lifecycle's claim that recovery 'stays with BindingStore' — corrected.\n- the dc-live-oracle label-writer census, which greps SOURCE TEXT keyed on FILE NAME and named binding_store.py as the third colon-form writer — retargeted to binding_recovery.py after verifying exactly three production writers remain.\n- this epic's own architectural-constraint list, whose 'after configuration snapshot AND before remote fetch' conjunction describes an EMPTY window: _load_snapshots constructs the store, runs the staleness gate and performs the FIRST remote fetch in one call, while bind_operation_runtime composes afterwards. Recorded as a comment rather than a description edit, deliberately, so the epic's signed attestation did not go stale. The pre-observation half was kept because it is load-bearing; the snapshot conjunct was dropped because completion reads only local binding state.\n\nACCEPTED (no action, with reason):\n- _env_int's minimum clamp is behaviourally UNOBSERVABLE through note_absent, because the counter increments before the comparison, so grace 0 or -3 retires on the first 404 exactly as a clamped 1 does. No test was manufactured for an unobservable difference — that would assert internal structure. Moot now regardless: RP-04 C3g cut that read to config.resolve_absent_retire_grace and deleted _env_int.\n- reconcile.py finishing at 799/800, one line of headroom. Accepted rather than pre-emptively extracted: the extraction belongs to whoever needs the space (RP-03 S5), and insectile-modest-martin guards the ordering constraint through it. Extracting speculatively here would have exceeded S3's scope.\n- Held-out artifacts under /tmp/rp02-heldout/ were not preserved durably. Accepted: every discriminator is now committed in the landed test suites, so the scratch copies have no remaining value.\n\nPROCESS LESSONS worth carrying (recorded in session log maxixe-ironic-kiskadee): 'make lint' does NOT cover every Verified gate — check_comment_hygiene.py, make config-check, check_verify_gate_parity.py, check_dco_identity.py, check_criteria_vocabulary.py, check_skill_frontmatter.py, zizmor and actionlint all run as separate CI steps; and a subsystem-scoped regression run misses repo-wide guards living outside the subsystem tree. Also: no source comment in this repo may cite a commit SHA — cite the TICKET — which matters for an epic whose whole method is characterising pre-extraction behaviour."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787169367786588001,"uuid":"28089190-aa9e-48b7-b75d-8fee95104591"}